.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_REG_GENERATION 3 "2026-07-13" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_reg_generation \- caller-visible identity token for a registration's fd
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

uint64_t ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns a value that only grows and is unique within one loop. reg
belongs to an fd, or to the bridge of a queue or a ccol_channel, and the
value is minted once, when ccol_event_loop_add() first registers that
fd and creates its entry. Every registration on that fd shares the
value for as long as the fd lives. That includes ccol_event_loop_modify()
calls, since flipping the direction keeps the same fd and the same
connection behind it, and it includes a read and a write registration
on the same fd, which share one generation because they stand for one
logical connection.

The value is for a caller's own defensive bookkeeping when fd numbers
get reused. A caller that keeps a connection object across several
asynchronous steps can stamp it with the generation read right after
ccol_event_loop_add() returns and compare that stamp with a fresh read
later on. A mismatch means that the fd number was closed and an
unrelated new connection has taken it, not the logical connection the
caller had in mind.

Basic correctness doesn't depend on the generation. The dispatch path
always checks that a registration is live before it calls a callback,
whether or not anybody calls this function. With more than one reactor
thread, the single poller thread collects readiness and a separate
dispatch worker runs the callback afterwards, so a registration can
disappear between those two steps of a batch, because it was removed or
a brand new registration reused its fd. The loop then skips it safely
at dispatch time, never dispatches it wrongly and never causes a
use-after-free. The generation only adds application bookkeeping on
top of that guarantee.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  ccol_event_loop reg belongs to.
reg   Registration to query.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The generation value. 0 when reg is CCOL_EVENT_REG_NULL or fails
to resolve for another reason, such as an earlier removal, and also
when loop is CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_NULL, a stale handle, or a handle that
has already been destroyed. A real registration never has generation 0,
because the counter starts at 1.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
You can call it at any time with any ccol_event_reg value that
ccol_event_loop_add ever returned for this loop, even one that has been
removed, earlier on this thread or by another thread racing with this
call. reg is resolved against loop's registration table before anything
is dereferenced, so a stale or removed handle is always caught and
reported as generation 0, never as a use-after-free.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call from any thread at any time, including while a dispatch,
a ccol_event_loop_modify() or a ccol_event_loop_remove() runs.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct conn {
    ccol_event_reg reg;
    uint64_t generation;
} conn_t;

conn_t *c = malloc(sizeof(*c));
if (!c) { perror("malloc"); exit(1); }
/* A callback of this registration can run before the next two stores,
   so it takes the handle from its reg parameter, never from c->reg. */
c->reg = ccol_event_loop_add(loop, ccol_selectable_from_fd(fd, ccol_select_read),
                        handlers, c, NULL);
c->generation = ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(loop, c->reg);

/* ... later, from another asynchronous step that holds a conn_t* ... */
if (ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(loop, c->reg) != c->generation) {
    /* stale: something closed this fd number and reused it */
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_add (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_modify (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_remove (3)
